{{!--
  /freestyle/sets — Set Encyclopedia, the one set page.

  The set list first: each set is its name, linking to its detail page, and
  one line saying what it is. The long notation lives on the detail page,
  never here. Below the list: how sets are built, the uptime reinterpretation
  ladders, Holden's compilation reproduced verbatim as an archive, and the
  sources. The retired /freestyle/compositional-sets and
  /freestyle/sets/reference addresses redirect to sections of this page, so
  the section ids come from the service and stay stable.
--}}
{{! The shared page hero, the same one the family detail page and the net
    landing use. The eyebrow's colour is written for this panel's dark
    background; on the page background it renders white on white. }}
<div class="hero hero-sm">
  <div>
    <p class="hero-eyebrow">{{page.eyebrow}}</p>
    <h1>{{page.title}}</h1>
    <p class="hero-subtitle">{{content.intro}}</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="wrapper">

  {{#if content.contents.length}}
  {{! One anchor per section, in render order. Reuses the shared
      .glossary-mini-toc styling. }}
  <nav class="glossary-mini-toc" aria-label="In this encyclopedia">
    <span class="glossary-mini-toc-label">In this encyclopedia:</span>
    <ol class="glossary-mini-toc-list">
      {{#each content.contents}}
      <li><a href="#{{anchorId}}">{{label}}</a></li>
      {{/each}}
    </ol>
  </nav>
  {{/if}}

  <p><a class="action-link" href="{{content.crossLinks.dictionarySetHref}}">{{content.crossLinks.dictionarySetLabel}}</a></p>

  {{#each content.subtypeSections}}
  <section class="sets-encyclopedia-subtype" id="set-subtype-{{key}}" aria-label="{{label}}">
    <div class="sets-encyclopedia-subtype-head">
      <h2>{{label}}</h2>
      <span class="sets-encyclopedia-subtype-count">{{count}}</span>
    </div>
    {{#if intro}}
    <p class="sets-encyclopedia-subtype-intro">{{intro}}</p>
    {{/if}}
    <ul class="dict-trick-row-stack" role="list">
      {{#each cards}}
      <li><article class="dict-trick-row" id="enc-set-{{slug}}" data-set-slug="{{slug}}">
        <div class="dict-trick-row-head">
          <a class="dict-trick-row-title" href="{{detailHref}}">{{displayName}}</a>
        </div>
        <p class="sets-encyclopedia-card-movement">{{compactMovement}}</p>
      </article></li>
      {{/each}}
    </ul>
    {{#if nextSubtypeAnchor}}
    {{! Read-next footer — forward pointer to the next rendered
        subtype. Suppresses on the last section. Reuses the shared
        .glossary-section-next styling. }}
    <p class="glossary-section-next">Next: <a href="#{{nextSubtypeAnchor}}">{{nextSubtypeLabel}}</a>, {{nextSubtypeTagline}}.</p>
    {{/if}}
  </section>
  {{/each}}

  {{! The sections below the list keep the content-section container they had
      on the pages they came from, so their prose keeps the body colour that
      container gives it. }}
  <section class="content-section" id="{{content.sectionIds.howSetsAreBuilt}}" aria-label="How Sets Are Built">
    <div class="section-heading"><h2>How Sets Are Built</h2></div>
    <p>
      Named freestyle sets compose from a small grammar. Notation work
      originating with Ben Job (1995) proposes that every set can be
      expressed as a sequence of set-type, side, direction, and dexterity
      tokens. The dictionary's operational notation system descends from
      that grammar.
    </p>

    <figure class="compositional-sets-canonical-formula">
      <code>{{content.howSetsAreBuilt.canonicalFormula}}</code>
      <figcaption>The canonical base shape.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p class="compositional-sets-softener">{{content.howSetsAreBuilt.softenerNote}}</p>

    <p>
      Each set prefix describes the arc of the bag from plant to catch. The notation uses four
      components: the <strong>set type</strong> (TOE, CLIP, or SET), the <strong>side</strong>
      (SAME or OP: the same leg as the most recent side-bearing component, or the opposite leg), the <strong>direction</strong>
      (IN or OUT), and optional <strong>modifiers</strong> ([DEX] for dexterity, [BOD] for body
      move). A &ldquo;&gt;&rdquo; between components means the next element follows before the catch.
    </p>
    <p>
      Example: <code>Pixie = TOE &gt; SAME IN [DEX] &gt;</code>, toe set, dexterity on the same
      side inward arc.
    </p>

    <p class="compositional-sets-premise-bridge">
      Within that scope, several named "sets" turn out to be recognizable
      trick structures under a different reading: blurry reads as stepping
      with a paradox second dex. Miraging is retained as descriptive
      mirage-family language for the inward standalone movement, not a launch
      set; the same inward movement realized with a set role is quantum. The
      structural relationships are explored below.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section" id="{{content.sectionIds.uptime}}" aria-label="Uptime Reinterpretation">
    <div class="section-heading"><h2>Uptime Reinterpretation</h2></div>
    <p class="compositional-sets-ladders-intro">
      Several named sets read as trick structures performed uptime. The
      ladders below show the relationship between a set and its
      trick-structural source. Where Holden's compilation and later
      platform analysis disagree, both readings are recorded.
    </p>

    {{#each content.ladders}}
      <article
        id="{{this.anchorId}}"
        class="compositional-ladder{{#if this.conflictNote}} compositional-ladder--conflict{{/if}}"
      >
        <header class="compositional-ladder-header">
          <p class="compositional-ladder-set-name">{{this.setName}}</p>
          <code class="compositional-ladder-set-notation">{{this.setNotation}}</code>
        </header>
        <p class="compositional-ladder-reinterpretation">&rarr; {{this.reinterpretation}}</p>
        <ol class="compositional-ladder-steps">
          {{#each this.steps}}
            <li class="compositional-ladder-step"><code>{{this}}</code></li>
          {{/each}}
        </ol>
        <p class="compositional-ladder-source"><span class="compositional-ladder-source-label">Source:</span> {{this.sourceCitation}}</p>
        {{#if this.conflictNote}}
          <p class="compositional-ladder-conflict">{{this.conflictNote}}</p>
        {{/if}}
      </article>
    {{/each}}
  </section>

  {{! Holden's compilation, verbatim: its own names, groupings and formulas,
      which may differ from the platform's. }}
  <section class="content-section" id="{{content.sectionIds.holden}}" aria-label="Historical Move-Set Reference">
    <div class="section-heading"><h2>Historical Move-Set Reference</h2></div>
    <p>
      This section reproduces a historical community compilation of freestyle move sets and
      notation. Its names, classifications, and formulas are preserved as source evidence
      and may differ from current platform doctrine.
    </p>
    <p class="source-note">{{content.holden.provenance.sourceNote}}</p>
    <p>{{content.holden.provenance.groupingNote}}</p>

    <section>
    <h3>Basic Sets</h3>
    <div class="moves-table-wrap">
      <table class="moves-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Set Name</th><th>Notation</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {{#each content.holden.basicSets}}
          <tr id="{{anchorId}}">
            <td>{{#if trickHref}}<a href="{{trickHref}}">{{label}}</a>{{else}}{{label}}{{/if}}</td>
            <td>{{#if notation}}<code>{{notation}}</code>{{/if}}</td>
          </tr>
          {{/each}}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section>
    <h3>Spinning Variants</h3>
    <div class="moves-tag-list">
      {{#each content.holden.spinningVariants}}
      <span class="moves-tag" id="{{anchorId}}">{{#if trickHref}}<a href="{{trickHref}}">{{label}}</a>{{else}}{{label}}{{/if}}</span>
      {{/each}}
    </div>
    </section>

    <section>
    <h3>Whirl / Swirl Variants</h3>
    <div class="moves-tag-list">
      {{#each content.holden.whirlSwirlVariants}}
      <span class="moves-tag" id="{{anchorId}}">{{#if trickHref}}<a href="{{trickHref}}">{{label}}</a>{{else}}{{label}}{{/if}}</span>
      {{/each}}
    </div>
    </section>

    <section>
    <h3>UNS Set Variants</h3>
    <div class="moves-tag-list">
      {{#each content.holden.unsVariants}}
      <span class="moves-tag" id="{{anchorId}}">{{#if trickHref}}<a href="{{trickHref}}">{{label}}</a>{{else}}{{label}}{{/if}}</span>
      {{/each}}
    </div>
    </section>

    <section>
    <h3>Antisymposium</h3>
    <div class="moves-tag-list">
      {{#each content.holden.antisymposium}}
      <span class="moves-tag" id="{{anchorId}}">{{#if trickHref}}<a href="{{trickHref}}">{{label}}</a>{{else}}{{label}}{{/if}}</span>
      {{/each}}
    </div>
    </section>

    <section>
    <h3>Components</h3>
    <p>These are modifiers or partial sets, not standalone set prefixes.</p>
    <div class="moves-tag-list">
      {{#each content.holden.components}}
      <span class="moves-tag" id="{{anchorId}}">{{#if trickHref}}<a href="{{trickHref}}">{{label}}</a>{{else}}{{label}}{{/if}}</span>
      {{/each}}
    </div>
    </section>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section" id="{{content.sectionIds.sources}}" aria-label="Sources and attribution">
    <div class="section-heading"><h2>Sources &amp; Attribution</h2></div>
    <p>
      Notation grammar originates with Ben Job's 1995 proposal to the
      footbag discussion list. The compilation above is Chris Holden's
      community-curated list (circa 2003), preserved verbatim in the
      <a href="#{{content.sectionIds.holden}}">Historical Move-Set Reference</a>.
      Holden's compilation is a community archive, not platform-canonical
      doctrine.
    </p>
  </section>

</div>
